---
/**
 * Who writes this, how each idea is measured, what the four confidence words mean, and
 * what AI did and did not do. One page, because a reader deciding whether to trust a
 * site does it once.
 */
import Hero from "~/components/Hero.astro";
import Verdict from "~/components/Verdict.astro";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import { REPO_URL } from "~/lib/site";

const WORDS = [
  {
    word: "Settled",
    gloss: "Arithmetic, a contract, or a tax rule. The only question left is whether you do it.",
  },
  {
    word: "Probably",
    gloss:
      "Measured, larger than the smallest thing the test could have seen, and it held up when I tried to break it. The size is a range, not a point.",
  },
  {
    word: "Too close to call",
    gloss: "The range covers real help and real harm. Not a yes and not a no.",
  },
  {
    word: "No",
    gloss: "Measured, and it cost more than it earned, or it failed a test set in advance.",
  },
] as const;
---

<Base
  title="About this site"
  description="Who writes it, how each idea is measured, what the four confidence words mean, and what AI did and did not do."
  asOf={null}
>
  <Hero
    title="About this site"
    lede="One person writes this. I am Sean Flanagan, a software engineer in the United States, and I do this in my own time."
  />

  <div class="prose">
    <p>I hold no financial licence and no credential of any kind, and nobody reviews a page before it goes up.
    Nothing pays for the site: no advertising, no affiliate links, no sponsors, and no money from any fund
    company, broker or data provider. Whatever I own of the funds named here is stated on
    <a href="/disclosures/">the disclosures page</a>, and I will not hold anything I would not disclose there.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>How the measuring works</h2>

    <p>Every idea on this site is tested inside a whole portfolio, not on its own. The question is never
    &ldquo;did gold go up&rdquo; but &ldquo;did adding gold to this portfolio, paid for by selling some of this
    portfolio, leave you better off than a cheap index fund holding the same mix&rdquo;. Fund fees and trading
    costs are charged inside every test, because an idea that only works before costs does not work.</p>

    <p>A result is only trusted when the data could actually have told the difference. Returns bounce around,
    and before running a test you can work out how large an effect would have to be for the history to show it.
    If the measured gap is smaller than that, the test proves nothing in either direction, and the page says
    so.</p>

    <p>The test is written down before it runs: which years, which funds, what it is compared against, and what
    counts as a pass. Then it runs, and the answer goes up whether or not it is the one I hoped for.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>The four confidence words</h2>

    <p>Every claim here carries one of these.</p>

    <ul class="word-list">
      {
        WORDS.map(({ word, gloss }) => (
          <li>
            <Verdict word={word} />
            <span>{gloss}</span>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <p>&ldquo;Too close to call&rdquo; is the most common verdict here and the one most likely to be misread. It
    is not a polite no. It means the history is too short or too noisy to answer, and the page says what would
    change that.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>What AI did</h2>

    <p>AI models did most of the calculating and most of the first drafting. I check every number against its
    source and rewrite the words. Every measured result points at a record carrying its
    value, its range, the date it was read, and where it came from, and the site refuses to build if that source
    has moved. The models have been wrong here, sometimes by a lot, and every error that changed a conclusion is
    on <a href="/corrections/">the corrections page</a> with its date and what caught it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>What this site will never do</h2>

    <p>It will never answer a question about your money, not by email, not privately, and not with the names
    changed. I do not know your tax bracket, your job, or how you behaved the last time your account fell by a
    third, and a general answer dressed as a personal one is worse than none.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>Checking the work</h2>

    <p>The code and the data behind every page are public at
    <a href={REPO_URL} rel="noopener noreferrer">the source</a>, including the history of what changed. If you
    find an error, open an issue there with the number, its source, and the sentence you think it contradicts.
    You do not need to be polite about it.</p>

    <p>Nothing here has been checked by a securities lawyer, a tax adviser or an accountant. The tax arithmetic
    assumes a US federal individual investor and ignores every state. <a href="/disclaimer/">The disclaimer</a>
    says the rest.</p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .word-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .word-list > li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 9.5rem 1fr;
    gap: 0.5rem 1rem;
    align-items: baseline;
    padding: 0.6rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .word-list > li:last-child {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  @media (width < 32rem) {
    .word-list > li {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0.25rem;
    }
  }
</style>
